@startuml client_send_queue_arm_activity
title ClientSendQueue - arming queued send dispatch

start
:ArmIfNeeded();
note right
    Entry: the caller holds lifetime_.mutex_.
end note
if (queue_ is empty or !lifetime_.IsAccepting()?) then (yes)
    :No pending IPC messages, or stopping has closed admission;
    :Return without scheduling a client-thread command;
    stop
else (no)
    :Pending IPC messages require progress;
endif
if (waiting_for_reply_ is engaged?) then (yes)
    :An existing drain or request already owns progress;
    note right
        Empty callback marker: a drain command is queued in the
        engine's client-thread command queue, queue processing is active,
        or a direct ordered one-way sender owns progress.
        Non-empty callback: a request owns the next reply completion.
        Neither case needs another drain command here.
    end note
    :Return without changing the slot or adding an engine command;
    stop
else (no)
    :No request or drain owns progress;
endif
:Set waiting_for_reply_ to an empty ReplyCallback marker;
:Invoke schedule_drain_();
note right
    When the engine executes the queued callback:
    acquire lifetime_.mutex_ with a unique lock,
    then call DrainUnderLock(lock).
    This continuation is separate from the arming call.
end note
:Return with the caller's lock still held;
stop

legend
    queue_ is the FIFO of pending IPC messages and their reply callbacks.
    The engine command queue schedules execution of async_send_command_, not each message.
    The marker engages the optional slot without registering a reply callback.
    Slot engagement is not a direct test of engine command-queue membership.
    A pending request callback is never overwritten by this helper.
    Direct-error recovery releases its slot before requesting queue progress.
    The facade actor enqueues its embedded command with connection ownership.
    Dispatch decides when to reset the marker or replace it with a reply callback.
endlegend

@enduml